<p>The way we heat, power, and maintain our homes has never been under greater scrutiny. With energy bills at historic highs, climate commitments requiring a rapid transition away from fossil fuels, and government regulations steadily raising the bar for property energy efficiency, sustainable living has shifted from a lifestyle choice to a mainstream priority. Whether you're a homeowner looking to reduce your carbon footprint and energy bills, or a landlord navigating incoming EPC requirements, the case for making your home more eco-friendly has never been stronger.</p>

<p>The good news is that sustainable living doesn't have to mean radical changes or huge upfront investment. Many of the most impactful improvements are surprisingly affordable, and several are even free. In this guide, Ark Housing walks you through the most effective eco-friendly housing tips, from quick wins to longer-term investments.</p>

<h2>1. Insulation: The Foundation of Energy Efficiency</h2>

<p>Heat loss through poorly insulated walls, roofs, and floors is the single biggest source of energy waste in UK homes. According to the Energy Saving Trust, a quarter of heat in an uninsulated home escapes through the roof, and around a third escapes through the walls. Addressing these losses through proper insulation is therefore the highest-impact action most homeowners can take.</p>

<p>Loft insulation is typically the cheapest and most effective starting point — a 270mm depth of mineral wool insulation can save up to £310 per year on energy bills and typically pays for itself in under two years. Cavity wall insulation (for properties built after the 1920s with a gap between inner and outer walls) is similarly cost-effective, and the ECO4 scheme offers free installation for eligible households. For solid-walled properties — common in older UK housing stock — internal or external wall insulation is more complex and expensive but can save up to £400 per year on heating costs.</p>

